  "title": "",
  "body": "     Modulus and Argument of Complex Numbers and Their Conjugates    <p>1. Find the modulus \\(r\\) and argument \\(\\theta \\) of \\({@aa@}{@sigb@}{@bbi@}\\).<\/p> <p>Give your answers in exact form (in terms of square roots and \\(\\pi\\)). Recall that \\(\\sqrt{a}\\) is entered as sqrt(a) and \\(\\pi\\) is entered as %pi.<\/p> <p>\\(r = \\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]<\/p> <p>\\(\\theta = \\) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]<\/p> <p>2. Find the modulus \\(q\\) and argument \\(\\delta \\) of the complex conjugate of \\({@aa@}{@sigb@}{@bbi@}\\).<\/p> <p>Give your answers in exact form.<\/p> <p>\\(q = \\) [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]<\/p> <p>\\(\\delta = \\) [[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:prt4]]<\/p>    <h3>Worked Solution<\/h3> <p>1. The complex number in question is \\({@aa@}{@sigb@}{@bbi@}\\). If we consider it in the form \\(a + b\\cdot i\\), then \\(a = {@aa@}\\) and \\(b = {@bb\/i@}\\). So, the modulus of \\({@aa@}{@sigb@}{@bbi@}\\) is<\/p> <p>\\(r = \\left| {@aa@}{@sigb@}{@bbi@}\\right| = \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}\\)<\/p> <p>\\( = \\sqrt{\\left( {@aa@}\\right) ^2 + \\left( {@bb\/i@}\\right) ^2}\\)<\/p> <p>\\( = \\sqrt{ {@aa^2@} + {@(bb\/i)^2@}}\\)<\/p> <p>\\( = \\sqrt{{@aa^2+(bb\/i)^2@}}\\)<\/p> <p>\\( = {@ta1@}\\)<\/p> <br> <p>The complex number in question is \\({@aa@}{@sigb@}{@bbi@}\\). If we consider it in the form \\(a + b\\cdot i\\), then \\(a = {@aa@}\\) and \\(b = {@bb\/i@}\\).<\/p> <p>We first note that, in the Argand Diagram, \\({@aa@}{@sigb@}{@bbi@}\\) lies in quadrant number {@quad@}, which means that the argument \\(\\theta \\) is within the range \\({@lb@}&lt;\\theta &lt; {@ub@}\\).<\/p> So, \\(\\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left( \\frac{{@abs(bb\/i)@}}{{@abs(aa)@}}\\right) \\)<p><\/p> <p> \\(= \\tan^{-1}\\left( {@abs(bb\/i)\/abs(aa)@}\\right) \\)<\/p> <p> \\(= {@atan(abs(bb\/i)\/aa)@}\\).<\/p> <p>2. So, considering the symmetry of the unit circle, for \\({@aa@}{@sigb@}{@bbi@}\\), \\(\\theta = {@ta2@}\\).<\/p> <br> <p>The modulus of a complex number and its conjugate are the same as the distance from the origin in the argand diagram will not change. So, \\(q = r = {@ta3@}\\).<\/p> <br> <p>In the Argand Diagram, the complex conjugate of a number is its reflection on the \\(x-\\)axis. Therefore, its argument is the negative of the argument of the original number. In this case, \\(\\delta = -\\theta = {@ta4@}\\).<\/p>   1  0.1  0    2025073100    tt:rand([3,4,6]); dumset:{}; for i:1 thru 2*tt do if gcd(i,tt)=1 then dumset:adjoin(i,dumset); dumlist:listify(dumset); pp:rand(dumlist); arg:pp*%pi\/tt; mod:if tt=4 then rand([1,2,6,10,sqrt(2),2*sqrt(2),3*sqrt(2)\/*,sqrt(6),sqrt(3)*\/]) else rand([1,2,4,5,7,sqrt(3),2*sqrt(3),4*sqrt(3)\/*,sqrt(2),sqrt(6)*\/]); cc:cos(arg); ss:sin(arg); aa:mod*cc; bb:mod*ss*i; sigb:if mod*ss<0 then \"-\" else \"+\"; bbi:if cabs(bb)=i then \"i\" else cabs(bb)*i; ta1:mod; ta2:if arg>%pi then arg-2*%pi else arg; ta3:mod; ta4:-ta2; \/*Other variables*\/ lba:if aa<0 then \"(\" else \"\"; rba:if aa<0 then \")\" else \"\"; lbb:if bb\/i<0 then \"(\" else \"\"; rbb:if bb\/i<0 then \")\" else \"\"; quad:if aa>0 then if bb\/i>0 then 1 else 4 else if bb\/i>0 then 2 else 3; if quad = 1 then (lb:0,ub:%pi\/2) elseif quad = 2 then (lb:%pi\/2,ub:%pi) elseif quad=3 then (lb:-%pi,ub:%pi\/2) else (lb:%pi\/2,ub:0);       <p>Complex number: \\({@aa@}{@sigb@}{@bbi@}\\)<\/p> <p>\\(r = {@ta1@}\\) <p>\\(\\theta = {@ta2@} \\) <p>\\(q = {@ta3@} \\) <p>\\(\\delta = {@ta4@} \\)      1  0  0   <span style=\"font-size: 1.5em; color:green;\"><i class=\"fa fa-check\"><\/i><\/span> Correct answer, well done.    <span style=\"font-size: 1.5em; color:orange;\"><i class=\"fa fa-adjust\"><\/i><\/span> Your answer is partially correct.    <span style=\"font-size: 1.5em; color:red;\"><i class=\"fa fa-times\"><\/i><\/span> Incorrect answer.   .  *10  none  1  i  cos-1  lang  [  0    ans1  algebraic  ta1  15  1  0   0    1  0  0  1  3     ans2  algebraic  ta2  15  1  0   0    1  0  0  1  3     ans3  algebraic  ta3  15  1  0   0    1  0  0  1  3     ans4  algebraic  ta4  15  1  0   0    1  0  0  1  3     prt1  1.0000000  1  1      0   AlgEquiv  ans1  ta1   0  =  1   -1  prt1-1-T     =  0   -1  prt1-1-F        prt2  1.0000000  1  1      0   AlgEquiv  ans2  ta2   0  =  1   2  prt2-1-T     =  0   1  prt2-1-F       1   AlgEquiv  ans2  arg   0  =  0.5   2  prt2-2-T   <p>Your answer is correct but not in the conventional argument range of \\(-\\pi &lt; \\theta \\leq \\pi\\).<\/p> <p><\/p>   -  0   2  prt2-2-F       2   AlgEquiv  quad  1   0  +  0   -1  prt2-3-T     -  0   -1  prt2-3-F        prt3  1.0000000  1  1      0   AlgEquiv  ans3  ta3   0  =  1   -1  prt3-1-T     =  0   -1  prt3-1-F        prt4  1.0000000  1  1      0   AlgEquiv  ans4  ta4   0  =  1   -1  prt4-1-T     =  0   -1  prt4-1-F       1204635627  2125435186  1321869247  1407393607  921228700  1322123396  724125201  1621348486  1272414060  1632824363  1018467456  1943242548  1171990202  590106565  1609760285  332679522  2145582676  1393163970  486349708  999717798  2146910560  1629321053  240961756  72821631  207671856  1843375177  527801812  2013803900  1774315859  387576095  1618115883  72409879  741672489  1322410832  724975140  2105934495  1788013521  863421824  133806463  430928451  71970050  566408057  143503053  1742024786  666962865   1    ans1  ta1    ans2  ta2    ans3  ta3    ans4  ta4    prt1  1.0000000  0.0000000  prt1-1-T    prt2  1.0000000  0.0000000  prt2-3-F    prt3  1.0000000  0.0000000  prt3-1-T    prt4  1.0000000  0.0000000  prt4-1-T        Add review   "
