  "id": "subsec-polar-form-of-a-complex-50",
  "level": "2",
  "url": "sec-the-set-of-complex-numbers.html#subsec-polar-form-of-a-complex-50",
  "type": "Checkpoint",
  "number": "2.2.37",
  "title": "",
  "body": "     Conversion to Polar Form Complex Number z = x + yi    <p>Write the complex number \\(z={@x@}+{@y@}i\\) in polar form using the argument \\(\\theta \\) \"the angle measured from the positive real axis.<\/p> <p><em>Note: \\({@%i @}\\) can be typed as <code>%i<\/code> .<\/em><\/p> <p>r = [[input:ans1]] and \\(\\theta\\) = [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans1]][[validation:ans2]]<\/p> <p>z = [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]][[feedback:prt1]]<\/p>    <h3>Worked Solution<\/h3> <p>The given complex number is \\({@x@}{@y@}i\\)<\/p> <p><strong>Step 1:<\/strong> We calculate the modulus (magnitude) of the complex number using the formula: \\(|z| = \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}\\) for any complex number \\(z= a+b.i \\)<\/p> <p>So, for our complex number the modulus is: \\(\\displaystyle r=\\sqrt{({@x@}^2)+({@y@}^2)}\\)<\/p> <p>Modulus r={@r@}<\/p> <p><\/p> <p><strong>Step 2:<\/strong> The argument \\( \\theta \\) of a complex number can be found using the formula:<\/p> <p>\\( \\displaystyle \\theta =tan^-1 \\left( \\frac{b}{a}\\right) \\implies tan^-1 \\left( \\frac{{@y@}}{{@x@}}\\right) \\) (Note: The angle needs to be in radians)<\/p> <p>\\(\\theta ={@arg@}\\)<\/p> <p><\/p> <p><strong>Step 3:<\/strong> The polar form of the complex number is given by \\(z=r(\\cos\\theta+i\\,\\sin\\theta)\\)<\/p> <p>Substituting the values of \\(r\\) and \\(\\theta\\) into the formula: \\( z = {@r@}\\left(\\cos{@arg@}+i\\, \\sin {@arg@}\\right)\\)<\/p>   1  0.1  0    2025012100    a: 1+rand(10); b: a*sqrt(3); z: -1*a-b*%i; x:realpart(z); y:imagpart(z); r: cabs(z); az: atan(y\/x); arg: %pi + carg(z); pz: r*%e^(%i*arg); ta: pz; simp:false; ta2: r*(cos(arg)+%i*sin(arg));       <p>The complex number \\(z={@x@}{@y@}i\\) in polar form using the argument.<\/p> <p><strong>Give your solution in \\(\\pi\\) radians, or in the form \\(z=re^{i\\theta}\\).<\/strong><\/p> <p>\\(r ={@r@}\\) and \\(\\theta ={@arg@}\\)<\/p> <p>\\(z ={@ta@}\\) <\/p>      1  0  0   <p>Correct answer, well done.<\/p>    <p>Your answer is partially correct.<\/p>    <p>Incorrect answer.<\/p>   .  *10  dot  1  i  cos-1  lang  [  0    ans1  numerical  r  8  1  0   0    1  0  0  1  3     ans2  algebraic  arg  8  1  0   0    1  0  0  1  3     ans3  algebraic  ta2  15  1  0   0    1  0  0  1  3     prt1  1.0000000  1  1   sans: ans1*%e^(%i*ans2);    0   AlgEquiv  ans1  r   0  =  0.3  0  1  prt1-1-T     =  0   1  prt1-1-F   <p>Check that you have calculated the value of \\(r\\) correctly.<\/p>     1   AlgEquiv  ans2  arg   0  +  0.5   3  prt1-2-T     -  0   2  prt1-2-F   <p>Check that you have correctly measured the angle from the positive real axis.<\/p>     2   AlgEquiv  ans3  sans   0  +  0.1   -1  prt1-3-T   <p>Your values for r and \\(\\theta\\) are incorrect, but you have correctly written the polar form using these values.<\/p>   -  0   -1  prt1-3-F       3   AlgEquiv  ans3  ta   0  +  0.2   -1  prt1-4-T     -  0   -1  prt1-4-F   <p>Your values for r and \\(\\theta\\) are correct, but you have not correctly written the polar form for the number. Check your syntax!<\/p>     800765924  1560814259  2110675066  973220628  334938587  1780174110  649209433  1133843212  1775401576  50388741   1    ans1  r    ans2  arg    ans3  ta    prt1  1.0000000  0.0000000  prt1-4-T        Add review   "
