  "number": "3.3.14",
  "title": "",
  "body": "     Inverse of a Function with Fractional Coefficients    <p><\/p><p>Find the inverse of \\(f(x)={@fx@}\\), giving your answer in the form \\(f^{-1}(x)=mx+c\\), where \\(m\\) and \\(c\\) are fractions in their simplest form.&nbsp;<\/p><p>\\(f^{-1}(x)=\\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]<br><\/p>      1  0.1  0    2025073100    aa:rand(8)+2; pm1:rand_with_prohib(-1,1,[0]); bb:pm1*(rand(8)+1); fx:aa*x+bb; ta:x\/aa-bb\/aa;    [[feedback:prt1]]    inverse of \\(f(x)={@fx@}\\) is {@ta@}      1  0  0   <span style=\"font-size: 1.5em; color:green;\"><i class=\"fa fa-check\"><\/i><\/span> Correct answer, well done.    <span style=\"font-size: 1.5em; color:orange;\"><i class=\"fa fa-adjust\"><\/i><\/span> Your answer is partially correct.    <span style=\"font-size: 1.5em; color:red;\"><i class=\"fa fa-times\"><\/i><\/span> Incorrect answer.   .  *10  dot  1  i  cos-1  lang  [  0    ans1  algebraic  ta  15  1  0   0    1  1  0  1  1     prt1  1.0000000  1  1      0   AlgEquiv  ans1  ta  x  0  =  1   -1  prt1-1-T     =  0   -1  prt1-1-F   <p>When calculating the inverse of a function \\(f(x)\\) we are finding a new&nbsp;function that will tell us which \\(x\\) value will give as a particular&nbsp;\\(y\\) value when we input it in \\(f\\). Therefore, in the case of \\(f(x)={@aa*x+bb@}\\), we write it as \\(y={@aa*x+bb@}\\) and invert the variables \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) to get \\(x={@aa*y+bb@}\\) and rearrange to get \\(y={@1\/aa@}x+{@-bb\/aa@}\\). Therefore the inverse of \\(f\\) is \\(f^{-1}(x) = {@1\/aa@}x+{@-bb\/aa@}\\).<\/p>     1814258227  1530227927  1299130557  102140904  1088401504  1676649398  526623092  973932729  584771351  1718338541  263091537  941919959  650814227  1284544782  1581590652  143897427  579582816  77151760  728544801  1148184446  1782613009   1  Test case assuming the teacher's input gets full marks.   ans1  ta    prt1  1.0000000  0.0000000  prt1-1-T        Add review   "
