  "body": "     Equations of a Line Passing Through Two Points    <p>Fill in the blanks<\/p> <br> To find the equation for the line passing through the points \\( P({@P_x@}, {@P_y@}, {@P_z@}) \\) and \\( Q({@Q_x@}, {@Q_y@}, {@Q_z@}) \\). <br>We can proceed as follows: <br><b>Step 1:<\/b> First, identify a vector that is parallel to the line, \\( (PQ) \\). <br><b>Recall:<\/b> A vector that is parallel to the line is \\( \\vec{PQ} \\) or \\( \\vec{QP} \\). <br> \\( \\vec{PQ}\\) ([[input:ans1]] , [[input:ans2]] , [[input:ans3]]) [[feedback:prtA]] <br> \\( \\quad \\) [[validation:ans1]] \\( \\quad \\) [[validation:ans2]] \\( \\quad \\) [[validation:ans3]] <br><b>Step 2:<\/b> Picking either point will give you parametric equations for the line. <br>Here, let use \\(P\\) and a parameter \\( \\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}\\). \\( \\alpha \\) is typed as <b>alpha<\/b> <br> Let the point \\( M(x, y) \\in (PQ) \\), so \\( \\vec{PM} \\) and \\( \\vec{PQ} \\) are colinear. <br> This means, there exists \\( \\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}\\), such that \\( \\vec{PM} = \\alpha \\vec{PQ} \\) <br> \\( \\vec{PM} = \\alpha \\vec{PQ} \\implies {@matrix([x-P_x],[y-P_y],[z-P_z])@}={@alpha*matrix([PQ_x],[PQ_y],[PQ_z])@}\\). Thus <br> \\( x= \\) [[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:prt4]] <br> \\( y= \\) [[input:ans5]] [[validation:ans5]] [[feedback:prt5]] <br> \\( z= \\) [[input:ans6]] [[validation:ans6]] [[feedback:prt6]] <br><b>Step 2:<\/b> Deduce the equations for the line passing through the points \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\). <br> The equations of the line are: <br> [[input:ans7]] = [[input:ans8]] = [[input:ans9]] [[feedback:prtB]] <br> \\( \\quad \\) [[validation:ans7]] \\( \\quad \\) [[validation:ans8]] \\( \\quad \\) [[validation:ans9]]    <h3><b>Worked solution<\/b><\/h3> <br><b>Step 1:<\/b> First, identify a vector that is parallel to the line, \\( (PQ) \\). <br><b>Recall:<\/b> A vector that is parallel to the line is \\( \\vec{PQ} \\) or \\( \\vec{QP} \\). <br> \\( \\vec{PQ} ({@PQ_x@}, {@PQ_y@}, {@PQ_z@}) \\) <br><b>Step 2:<\/b> Picking either point will give you parametric equations for the line. <br>Here, let use \\(P\\) and a parameter \\( \\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}\\). \\( \\alpha \\) is typed as <b>alpha<\/b> <br> Let the point \\( M(x, y) \\in (PQ) \\), so \\( \\vec{PM} \\) and \\( \\vec{PQ} \\) are colinear. <br> This means, there exists \\( \\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}\\), such that \\( \\vec{PM} = \\alpha \\vec{PQ} \\) <br> \\( \\vec{PM} = \\alpha \\vec{PQ} \\implies {@matrix([x-P_x],[y-P_y],[z-P_z])@}={@alpha*matrix([PQ_x],[PQ_y],[PQ_z])@}\\). Thus <br> \\( x= {@ta_4@} \\) <br> \\( y= {@ta_5@} \\) <br> \\( z= {@ta_6@} \\) <br><b>Step 2:<\/b> Deduce the equations for the line passing through the points \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\). <br> Since \\( x= {@ta_4@} \\) then \\( \\alpha = \\frac{{@x -P_x@}}{{@ta_1@}} \\) <br> Similarly \\( y= {@ta_5@} \\) then \\( \\alpha = \\frac{{@y -P_y@}}{{@ta_2@}} \\) <br> and \\( z= {@ta_6@} \\) then \\( \\alpha = \\frac{{@z -P_z@}}{{@ta_3@}} \\) <br> Therefore, the equations of the line are: <br> \\( {@equation1@}={@equation2@}={@equation3@} \\)   1  0.1  0    2023060500    \/*Defining the randomized points*\/ P_x: rand_with_step(-5,5,1); P_y: rand_with_step(-6,5,1); P_z: rand_with_step(-4,7,1); Q_x: rand_with_prohib(-7,7,[P_x]); Q_y: rand_with_prohib(-5,5,[P_y]); Q_z: rand_with_prohib(-4,4,[P_z]); PQ_x: Q_x-P_x; \/*abscissa of vector PQ*\/ PQ_y: Q_y-P_y; \/*ordinate of vector PQ*\/ PQ_z: Q_z-P_z; \/*cote of vector PQ*\/ \/*Model answers*\/ ta_1: PQ_x; ta_2: PQ_y; ta_3: PQ_z; ta_4: ta_1*alpha+P_x; ta_5: ta_2*alpha+P_y; ta_6: ta_3*alpha+P_z; equation1: (x -P_x)\/ta_1; equation2: (y -P_y)\/ta_2; equation3: (z -P_z)\/ta_3; ta_7: equation1; ta_8: equation2; ta_9: equation3;       <br> Find equations for the line passing through the points \\( P({@P_x@}, {@P_y@}, {@P_z@}) \\) and \\( Q({@Q_x@}, {@Q_y@}, {@Q_z@}) \\). <br> \\( \\vec{PQ} ({@PQ_x@}, {@PQ_y@}, {@PQ_z@}) \\) and \\( {@equation1@}={@equation2@}={@equation3@} \\)      1  0  0   <span style=\"font-size: 1.5em; color:green;\"><i class=\"fa fa-check\"><\/i><\/span> Correct answer, well done.    <span style=\"font-size: 1.5em; color:orange;\"><i class=\"fa fa-adjust\"><\/i><\/span> Your answer is partially correct.    <span style=\"font-size: 1.5em; color:red;\"><i class=\"fa fa-times\"><\/i><\/span> Incorrect answer.   .  *10  dot  1  i  cos-1  lang  [  0    ans1  algebraic  ta_1  3  1  0   0    1  0  0  1  3     ans2  algebraic  ta_2  3  1  0   0    1  0  0  1  3     ans3  algebraic  ta_3  3  1  0   0    1  0  0  1  3     ans4  algebraic  ta_4  7  1  0   0    1  0  0  1  3     ans5  algebraic  ta_5  7  1  0   0    1  0  0  1  3     ans6  algebraic  ta_6  7  1  0   0    1  0  0  1  3     ans7  algebraic  ta_7  7  1  0  x+?  0    1  0  0  1  3     ans8  algebraic  ta_8  7  1  0  y+?  0    1  0  0  1  3     ans9  algebraic  ta_9  7  1  0  z+?  0    1  0  0  1  3     prt4  1.0000000  1  1   \/*Node 1: Checks for the correct answer *\/    0   AlgEquiv  ans4  ta_4   0  =  1   -1  prt4-1-T     =  0   -1  prt4-1-F        prt5  1.0000000  1  1   \/*Node 1: Checks for the correct answer *\/    0   AlgEquiv  ans5  ta_5   0  =  1   -1  prt5-1-T     =  0   -1  prt5-1-F        prt6  1.0000000  1  1   \/*Node 1: Checks for the correct answer *\/    0   AlgEquiv  ans6  ta_6   0  =  1   -1  prt6-1-T     =  0   -1  prt6-1-F        prtA  1.0000000  1  1   \/*Node 1: Checks for the correct answer *\/ \/*Node 2: Checks for the correct answer *\/ \/*Node 3: Checks for the correct answer *\/    0   AlgEquiv  ans1  ta_1   0  =  1   1  prtA-1-T     =  0   -1  prtA-1-F       1   AlgEquiv  ans2  ta_2   0  +  1   2  prtA-2-T     -  0   -1  prtA-2-F       2   AlgEquiv  ans3  ta_3   0  +  1   -1  prtA-3-T     -  0   -1  prtA-3-F        prtB  1.0000000  1  1   \/*Node 1: Checks for the correct answer *\/ \/*Node 2: Checks for the correct answer *\/ \/*Node 3: Checks for the correct answer *\/    0   AlgEquiv  ans7  ta_7   0  =  1   1  prtB-1-T     =  0   -1  prtB-1-F   You should get {@equation1@}     1   AlgEquiv  ans8  ta_8   0  +  1   2  prtB-2-T     -  0   -1  prtB-2-F   You should get {@equation2@}     2   AlgEquiv  ans9  ta_9   0  +  1   -1  prtB-3-T     -  0   -1  prtB-3-F   You should get {@equation3@}     1802914176  1465638463  238125537  1592536690  538196688  48936883  274899448  577626938  538875319  1296965745  2076428081  169796795  1816961287  2112515952  1653796507  1361196275  1563289073  493908851  1880712811  932072382  1321886821  598392263  1012104112  1800398897  1515917369  472647823  449618620  456291192  494831175  1744545013  1148016298  522450093  197750852  1052281369  1710269159  1009252744  638143039  312274643  1802589900  2139293287  1389677108  745670502  897900822  429150431  561324765  1797195591  703736029  665674467  59401842  959704269  1053657381  819160901  1178893777  2137029221  2147075767  1369959943  174187942  720896597  1588107894  1142554108  1651806262  762674862  15784673  1055018105  417080537  1607160559  779523944  682061081  1319578654  474958781  552828734  1907873330  1662996465  415524673  434923896  1363547603  655149900  1786165731  267027964  1235318241  732184955  494618591  1694284382  627693737  1326352896  423995348  1224859846  967945823  1789468602  285877499  1929382857   1    ans1  ta_1    ans2  ta_2    ans3  ta_3    ans4  ta_4    ans5  ta_5    ans6  ta_6    ans7  ta_7    ans8  ta_8    ans9  ta_9    prt4  1.0000000  0.0000000  prt4-1-T    prt5  1.0000000  0.0000000  prt5-1-T    prt6  1.0000000  0.0000000  prt6-1-T    prtA  1.0000000  0.0000000  prtA-3-T    prtB  1.0000000  0.0000000  prtB-3-T        Add review   "
