  "number": "4.1.16",
  "title": "",
  "body": "     Finding Equation of a Line with Given Slope and Point    <p>Find an equation of the line that has a slope of {@slope@} and passes through the point ({@x_cor@},{@y_cor@}).<\/p> <p>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]][[feedback:prt1]]<\/p> <p><\/p>    <p><b><h3>Worked solution<\/h3><\/b><\/p> <p>Find an equation of the line that has a slope of {@slope@} and passes through the point ({@x_cor@},{@y_cor@}).<\/p> <p>We recall that the general form of the line equation is \\(y-y_1=m(x-x_1) \\) where \\(m\\) is the slope.<\/p> <p> Substituting {@y_cor@} for \\(y_1 \\), {@x_cor@} for \\(x_1 \\), and \\({@slope@}\\) for \\(m\\) we get;<\/p> <p>\\( \\begin{align} y{@if y_cor<0 then \"+\" else \"-\"@}{@abs(y_cor)@} & ={@slope@}(x{@if x_cor<0 then \"+\" else \"-\"@}{@abs(x_cor)@}) \\\\ {@y-y_cor@} &={@expand(slope*(x-x_cor))@}\\\\ y & = {@expand(slope*(x-x_cor)+y_cor)@} \\end{align}\\)<\/p>   1  0.1  0    2023060500    \/*Question variables*\/ slope: rand_with_prohib(-5,5,[0]); x_cor: rand_with_step(-5,5,1); y_cor:rand_with_step(-5,5,1); \/*Worked solution*\/ eqn1:y-y_cor=slope*(x-x_cor); eqn2: y=expand(slope*(x-x_cor)+y_cor); ta:solve(eqn1,y)[1];       <p>slope \\(={@slope@}\\)<\/p> <p>points: \\(({@x_cor@},{@y_cor@})\\)<\/p> <p>equation of the line :\\({@y-y_cor@}={@slope@}({@x-x_cor@}) \\)      1  0  0   <span style=\"font-size: 1.5em; color:green;\"><i class=\"fa fa-check\"><\/i><\/span> Correct answer, well done.    <span style=\"font-size: 1.5em; color:orange;\"><i class=\"fa fa-adjust\"><\/i><\/span> Your answer is partially correct.    <span style=\"font-size: 1.5em; color:red;\"><i class=\"fa fa-times\"><\/i><\/span> Incorrect answer.   .  *10  dot  1  i  cos-1  lang  [  0    ans1  algebraic  ta  10  1  0   0    1  0  0  1  3     prt1  1.0000000  1  1   \/*Node 2: Permutation of x1 and y1 during the comutation*\/ mistake1: y=expand(slope*(x-y_cor)+x_cor);    0   AlgEquiv  ans1  ta   0  =  1   -1  prt1-1-T     =  0   1  prt1-1-F       1   AlgEquiv  ans1  mistake1   0  =  1\/2   -1  prt1-2-T   <p>You interchanged the values of \\(x_{1}={@x_cor@}\\) and \\(y_{1}={@y_cor@}\\) in the formula of the equation of the given line during your computation<\/p>   -  0   -1  prt1-2-F       1927221910  1284095120  134767110  1509815683  192421947  461692857  149808191  1590976572  1753345576  1454903713  432621161  204547282  56776003  1140561990  450702398  344191295  648720485  981250432  1345120176  106113648  2125219377  1799013692  1821944810  1430249073  1092833897  1477140160  1629771636  249676957  830623890  1093918599  232335688  1572276930  143500300  1154647781  351307872  1664276434  1365968771  2017963117  1996882385  869307756  167620096  2088513366  578904191  1939489320  91797809  2142956807  884705366  11860031  299657259  1201331293  215770606  947238080  237971046  1878130574  1384918394  473964591  1013368659  2007432367  2013588595  1960835512  83976238  1869681979  1587258938  2029153581  1102894805  672885399  409307622  1281609986  1750257292  249292372  1953793263  1451821894  383272320  219138801  466163032  1307103685  2085151759  1329757231  279427800  839006663  547752547  31238745  575836022  2113836741  700127908  1713499945  1749348271  910954886  293443724  2097387589  2044561033  1764450120  2057714244  1248621361  1989493177  958861414  170462013  60156371  548625369  408054852   1    ans1  ta    prt1  1.0000000  0.0000000  prt1-1-T        Add review   "
